// `pure` on a method of a GENERIC class, refused BY NAME rather than by accident.
//
// `pure` over a type parameter is no problem in itself: purity is about what the answer depends on,
// and a parameter changes nothing about that. What is unsettled is **which copy owns the promise.**
// A method on a generic class is checked once per instantiation, so `Box<Int>` and `Box<String>`
// would each register the same `pure` claim under a different key, and nothing today checks that the
// copies agree. That is a question about monomorphised keys, not about purity.
//
// It is refused with its own sentence for a reason this project has paid for twice: a blanket
// refusal that happens to cover a case is how the `?` operator went its whole life unimplemented in
// one compiler, and how a `pure` method looked impossible when only the parser said so. A named
// refusal is a to-do a reader can act on; an accidental one is a gap nobody can see.
class Box<T> { held: T }

pure function (self: Box<T>) get() -> T {
    return self.held;
}

region r {
    let b: Box<Int> = Box { held: 5 };
    print(b.get());
}
